ized as a mythological/philosophical figure who undergoes crises, convalescence, and metamorphosis, embodying the drama of time and engaging with themes such as becoming-equal, the eternal return, and the transformative journey of existence. Taken together, Zarathustra serves as Nietzsche’s foundational alter-ego—an archetype through which satire, reversal, sign-interpretation, and the ethical implications of repetition and time are examined.", "DIONYSUS:DIONYSUS is a mythological figure—the Greek god of wine and theatre. In the text, DIONYSUS is described as not separating himself from Christ, highlighting overlapped identities among philosophical personae. He is also presented as the Greek god of wine and revelry, mentioned in contrast to Apollo within the discussion of depth, height, and surface. Together, these descriptions portray DIONYSUS as a multifaceted figure whose domains include wine, revelry, and theatre, who operates at the intersection of different religious and philosophical frameworks—spanning Christian imagery and classical Greek contrasts with Apollo in debates about depth, height, and surface.", "SOCRATES:SOCRATES is an ancient Greek philosopher who appears as an object of encounter in a discussion of sensibility and thought. In the same text, he is described as an ironist who operates in private through brief arguments, and as a figure indistinguishable from the Sophists. This combination is used to illustrate the difficulties of self-differentiation in a philosophical persona. The text even presents SOCRATES as not distinguishing himself from his sophist, highlighting the porous boundary between Socratic and Sophistic modes within that discourse. Taken together, these descriptions portray SOCRATES as a nuanced and debated figure whose philosophical stance is framed in terms of ambiguity and proximity to the Sophists, rather than a clear, singular boundary between himself and his intellectual counterparts.", "VAN GOGH:VAN GOGH (Vincent van Gogh) is described as a painter referenced in discussions of sensation and color in art, with Van Gogh's yellow highlighted as a key example in the text. The descriptions hail him for taking yellow to the limitless, illustrating this through the notion of the man-sunflower and the idea of a plane of infinite little commas. Together, these images show how color can express conceptual ideas beyond straightforward representation. The summary emphasizes van Gogh’s role in pushing the expressive potential of color and brushwork, using bold, saturated yellows to convey emotion, sensation, and abstraction. Through these metaphorical constructs—the man-sunflower and the plane of infinite little commas—the text portrays Van Gogh as a transformative figure in art who demonstrates that color can function as a vehicle for conceptual and perceptual exploration, reinforcing his influence on post-impressionist practice and theory. In essence, VAN GOGH is depicted as a pioneering painter whose distinctive use of yellow serves as a conduit for expansive ideas about perception, emotion, and the expressive capacity of color.", "MELVILLE:American writer (Herman Melville); in the text, cited as saying that great novelistic characters must be Originals, Unique, serving as an example in the discussion of concepts and personas.", "HEGEL:Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (HEGEL) is presented as a German philosopher whose work centers on dialectics and the dynamic, evanescent movement of becoming. In the passage, his ideas are linked to the concept of difference and repetition and to the infinite movement of evanescence, signaling a focus on how change and development emerge from internal contradictions. The text also offers a mathematical analogy attributed to Hegel, suggesting that variability in a function arises when one independent variable is raised to a higher power, thereby illustrating the relationship between a state of affairs and the system—an image of how parts relate to the whole within his thinking. It further characterizes Hegel’s philosophy as deeper than Leibniz’s, with an increased emphasis on the ground (Grund) within his system, especially in discussions of infinite representation and identity. Additionally, the passage links Hegel with dialectical theory and notes that the long historical distortion of the dialectic culminates with him, positioning him as a pivotal figure in the culmination or reckoning of dialectical development. Overall, Hegel is framed as a central German philosopher whose dialectical method, together with his handling of the infinite, grounding of concepts, and the dynamics of identity, contributes to a more profound conception of reality and its representation than earlier rationalist paradigms.", "MENDELEEV:Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev, renowned for creating the periodic table; referenced here via \"Mendeleyev's table\" in a discussion about carbon bifurcation and the emergence of organic matter.", "EDMUND HUSSERL:Edmund Husserl, German philosopher (1859–1938) who founded phenomenology and authored Ideas, a foundational text in the field, is described as seeing it through to the end by discovering, in non-numerical multiplicities or immanent perceptivo-affective fusional sets, the triple root of acts of transcendence through which the subject constitutes first a sensory world filled with objects, then an intersubjective world occupied by the other, and finally a common ideal world that will be occupied by scientific, mathematical, and logical formations.", "BADIOU:French philosopher who develops the notion of multiplicities, states of affairs, and the concept of the event in contemporary philosophy; he discusses how sets of functions and concepts organize virtual and actual states and the potentiality that drives events.", "PEGUY:PEGUY is described as one of the two thinkers who have gone farthest into the Event, identified as a philosopher exploring the Event. In the same text, PEGUY is referenced alongside Raymond Roussel, though no additional attributes are provided.", "EVENT:A philosophical concept described in the text as pure immanence, not the state of affairs, immaterial and real without being actual, and representing the 'meanwhile' in becoming", "MALLARME:Stéphane Mallarmé, a French symbolist poet, referenced in the text as counter-effectuating the event and associated with the term Mime as a conceptual persona)<|COMPLETE|>(\"entity\"", "PESSOA:The text mentions Pessoa in the same comparative context with Proust; identified as a writer involved in a discussion about procedures in the search for sensation", "CEZANNE:CEZANNE is described as a painter who foregrounds sensation and color as central to painting. The descriptions associate him with innate sensations in the experience of color, discuss the organization of color into planes, and refer to the architecture of painting as a structural concept in his work. In the cited text, Cézanne is noted as a painter who discusses coloring within the framework of painting and sensation, underscoring color as both a perceptual and architectural element of art.", "BACON:An artist referenced for his figures in the discussion of flesh and painting within phenomenology of art", "DUBUFFET:Dubuffet is an artist referenced in the passage as trying to identify a condition of art brut; his work is described as standing between architecture, sculpture, and painting, and he turns to the house for inspiration.)<|COMPLETE|>"], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_687ef298e34b08a676ebc89193856a7e44cd331ef7892265ab5c52b60e913f7c_v2"}